---
// v1.1 redesign: the real About page, replacing the placeholder body copy
// and photo-less layout that shipped with the site's skeleton. A reading
// page, not a section-banded one — one white band (surface-1, the same
// "reading body" treatment Article.astro's body uses), a title block, one
// photo space, and the body copy, all sharing one column (var(--measure))
// so nothing on the page floats at a random width relative to the text
// above or below it. rise-in/photo-settle on the title block and hero
// photo match every other page's opening block, not a new pattern.
//
// The sign-off pulls the name/title from the site's one shared author
// config (src/lib/author.ts) rather than hardcoding the string a second
// time — today that resolves to exactly the given copy, and stays correct
// automatically if the author config is ever updated at /keystatic.
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Prose from '../components/Prose.astro';
import { Image } from 'astro:assets';
import { getAuthor } from '../lib/author';
import aboutHero from '../assets/author/about-hero.jpg';

const author = await getAuthor();
---

<Base
  title="About"
  description="Why Cuerpo Coffee exists and who writes it: two decades of paying attention to coffee, starting from a tree on a family plot of land."
>
  <div class="about-band">
    <header class="container about__header rise-in">
      <h1>About</h1>
      <p class="about__standfirst">
        Fifteen years old, holding a red coffee cherry I didn't know the name for, on my
        father's land. That's where this started.
      </p>
    </header>

    <Image
      src={aboutHero}
      alt="Jorge Gomez, founder of Cuerpo Coffee"
      class="about__hero photo-settle"
      widths={[480, 768, 1170]}
      sizes="(min-width: 40rem) 40rem, 100vw"
      format="avif"
    />

    <div class="container about__body">
      <Prose>
        <p>
          I've had a cup of coffee every day since I was fourteen. For most of those years,
          that just meant drinking it. It took a coffee tree to make me actually pay
          attention.
        </p>
        <p>
          My father kept a piece of land outside the city — a small weekend house, and a
          much bigger stretch of trees: guava, lemon, apple, berries, mango, orange,
          potatoes, more flowers than I can name. A few coffee trees were mixed in among
          them. I noticed the red cherries on the branches before I understood what they
          were, and once I did, I couldn't leave it alone.
        </p>
        <p>
          My father walked me through the process himself — picking, pulping, drying, the
          parts most people never see because their coffee already arrives as a bag of brown
          beans. I processed my own batch. Then I bought a small roaster and roasted my first
          kilo. I was fifteen.
        </p>
        <p>
          That's not a story about becoming a professional. I didn't go work for a roaster,
          and I don't have a certification to point to. What I have is closer to twenty years
          of drinking coffee on purpose — comparing beans, changing variables, noticing what
          actually moves a cup from fine to good.
        </p>
        <p>
          Somewhere in there it stopped being about the caffeine. I built a small coffee bar
          in my own house, bought a real espresso machine and the accessories that go with
          it, and turned the first cup of the morning into something closer to a ritual than
          a habit. I don't drink it to wake up anymore. I drink it to slow down before the
          day asks anything of me.
        </p>
        <p>
          That's the whole premise of this site. Not expertise handed down from a café job,
          but two decades of paying attention, starting from a tree I couldn't identify at
          fifteen. If you're standing where I was — curious, slightly obsessed, not sure
          where to start — that's exactly who this is for.
        </p>
        <p class="about__signoff">{author.data.name} — {author.data.title}</p>
      </Prose>
    </div>
  </div>
</Base>

<style>
  /* Same "reading body" white band Article.astro's own body sits on —
     About is a reading page, not a section-banded one. */
  .about-band {
    background: var(--surface-1);
    padding-block: var(--space-8);
  }

  .about__header {
    max-width: var(--measure);
    margin-inline: auto;
    margin-bottom: var(--space-6);
  }

  .about__standfirst {
    font-size: var(--step-1);
    color: var(--espresso);
    margin-top: var(--space-4);
  }

  /* Deliberately capped to the same column width as the title/body above
     and below it, not stretched to the wider article-hero treatment —
     about-hero.jpg is a portrait-oriented photo (taller than wide), and
     forcing it to a landscape-hero width would either distort it or crop
     away the headroom/shoulders it was chosen for. Keeping it at the
     page's one reading column also means nothing on this page floats at
     an arbitrary width relative to the text around it. */
  .about__hero {
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
    max-width: var(--measure);
    height: auto;
    margin-inline: auto;
    margin-bottom: var(--space-8);
    border-radius: var(--radius-lg);
  }

  .about__body {
    max-width: var(--measure);
    margin-inline: auto;
  }

  /* padding-top + border-top, not margin-top: Prose's own `.prose > * + *`
     rule already gives this paragraph a margin-top like any other body
     paragraph — adding a second margin-top here would stack with it, the
     same class of bug flagged repeatedly elsewhere in this codebase
     (CLAUDE.md's session 3/4 fixes, the Reflection in-essay photo). */
  .about__signoff {
    padding-top: var(--space-6);
    border-top: 1px solid var(--hairline);
    font-weight: 700;
  }
</style>
